[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. The desk's own table shows two
causes in it, and only one of them had a ticket. Each now has its own:

- [luca-gugs/what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/1): **`parser`.** The
  shared parser reads every date month first, so a day-first tenant's dates come out with day and
  month swapped. App, email and the booking-v2 form.
- [luca-gugs/what-it-shows#3](https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/3): **`hint`.** The
  booking-v3 form hints `MM/DD/YYYY`, so people type the month first, then the form reads the date
  day first. Only reports that came in through that form.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. The queued parser fix covers #1 only. The booking-v3 form needs its own fix: #3.
</Callout>

## What the desk's table shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

How to read it against the tickets:

- **The `parser` row is #1. The `hint` row is #3.** Each ticket counts what its row counts. The
  `hint` row's "no ticket of its own" is what #3 answers.
- **The footnote stays with `parser`.** The desk files those reports by tenant and counts them
  there, so #1 counts them too.
- The "tagged as" column is intake's guess from the reporter's browser. It does not decide the
  cause; the tenant's booking form and the channel do.

## What this changes

- #4471 is not "known, one fix queued". The queued fix closes #1; #3 needs its own fix and its own
  owner.
- Merging on the subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) put both
  causes into one ticket. That decision stays as it is; a new record should replace it.

